text:
 Durable webhooks

 Experimental database deliveries

Studio Webhooks (/webhooks) supports committed database INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE subscriptions for the selected immutable branch. The existing maintenance worker now runs an authenticated webhook executor. This is an experimental integration, not completion of specification 1.1 or the email-alert, recovery-drill or push services.

Delivery is at least once, not exactly once. An HTTP 2xx means the receiver accepted a request—not that a downstream action completed. Cancellation, policy changes and revocation cannot retract an in-flight request.

 Create a subscription

1. Expose the table and only intended columns in Database.
2. Create an active service key with this immutable branch ID, this table and GET. primary scopes, publishable keys, revoked/expired keys and policy-bypass keys are rejected for webhooks.
3. Configure a SELECT policy for role service. Its server-derived sub is the service-key ID, not the raw key. Use the actual policy simulator; administrator access is not proof of integration access.
4. Open Webhooks, choose the key/table, enter a public HTTPS receiver, select events/columns and create. Include every primary key in the projection; hidden keys/columns are rejected.
5. Save the signing secret on the receiver. It is returned only on creation/rotation and is never listed. Studio shows it in an ephemeral secret dialog; it does not save it to browser storage. Copying it intentionally places it on your clipboard.

Creation starts at the current outbox checkpoint; older commits are not retroactively delivered. URLs cannot include credentials, query strings or fragments. Management reads require viewer access; create/state/rotation and Job Inbox replay require administrator-authorized, audited writes and the existing MFA rules.

 Authorization and checkpoints

The existing branch owner atomically advances each subscription checkpoint and enqueues encrypted reference jobs. Rolled-back database writes never appear. Duplicate production scans do not create duplicate jobs. A job contains subscription/sequence references, not captured protected row contents.

Immediately before dispatch the private API checks key validity, exact branch/table/GET scope and non-bypass configuration, then applies the actual current SELECT policy engine and column allowlist to the outbox image. Changed exposure/policies or revoked credentials prevent dispatch. Invisible rows are skipped; deletion/visibility-loss projections contain only subscribed keys. Dispatch preparation rejects expired leases. Restores remove subscriptions, pending jobs and Advisor acknowledgments rather than reviving integrations.

The outbox window remains 24 hours. A missed checkpoint sets resync_required, never silently advances it. Reconcile the gap before creating a new subscription. An expired source event cannot be resurrected through dead-letter replay. Pausing does not extend retention; paused or revoked deliveries are skipped, not confirmed delivered.

 Verify exact-body signatures

Headers are x-qb-event-id, x-qb-timestamp (Unix seconds) and x-qb-signature (v1=hex, with two signatures during rotation). Compute HMAC-SHA256 with the signing-secret string over:


timestamp + "." + eventId + "." + exact raw request body bytes


Do not JSON-parse/re-serialize before verification. Reject stale/future timestamps, bound input/header sizes and compare signatures in constant time. Example receiver helper using native Node crypto:


import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from 'node:crypto';

export function verifyWebhook(body: Uint8Array, headers: Headers, secret: string): boolean {
  if (body.byteLength  65_536) return false;
  const timestamp = headers.get('x-qb-timestamp') ?? '';
  const eventId = headers.get('x-qb-event-id') ?? '';
  const signatures = headers.get('x-qb-signature') ?? '';
  if (!/^\d{1,12}$/.test(timestamp) || !eventId || eventId.length  200 || signatures.length  256)
    return false;
  if (Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - Number(timestamp))  300) return false;
  const expected = createHmac('sha256', secret)
    .update(${timestamp}.${eventId}.).update(body).digest();
  return signatures.split(',').some(signature = {
    if (!/^v1=[a-f0-9]{64}$/.test(signature)) return false;
    return timingSafeEqual(expected, Buffer.from(signature.slice(3), 'hex'));
  });
}


After verification, atomically deduplicate eventId in the receiver's durable storage alongside its side effect. Keep IDs for at least the 30-day replay/history window. Rotating a secret creates a 24-hour dual-signature overlap; another rotation is blocked until that overlap expires. Rotate the receiver's verifier during the overlap, then discard the old secret. Replay keeps the original event ID but signs a fresh timestamp and currently authorized projection; consumers must tolerate projection changes.

 Delivery limits and diagnostics

At most 20 non-revoked subscriptions/branch, 100 listed subscriptions (active first), 1,000 retained subscription records and 200 produced jobs per owner call. Revoked subscription history expires after 30 days. Existing jobs cap payloads at 64 KiB and pending work at 10,000/branch. Queue-full failures roll back the producer checkpoint, log metadata-only backpressure and do not prevent scheduled cleanup/backups. Other producer failures remain errors, not successful maintenance.

The worker polls only registered branch markers, at most eight targets/page, two claims/target and 20 completions/20 seconds per tick; heavy requests can finish beyond the tick budget but remain individually bounded. Empty targets do not allocate every project owner. Executor-only owners retire before replacement; application owners remain alive. Leases default to 30 seconds; HTTPS/DNS requests have a ten-second deadline. Requests forward only signature/content headers, never cookies or worker/management credentials; response bodies are not persisted or logged.

Webhooks allow ten attempts within 24 hours, with bounded exponential backoff. Timeouts, connection failures, HTTP 408/429 and 5xx retry; other failures dead-letter. Redirects never follow. [Job Inbox](/docs/studio/job-inbox) shows attempts, controlled codes, cancel and explicit replay. PROVIDER_ACCEPTED is handoff; skip codes include KEY_REVOKED, KEY_SCOPE, NOT_AUTHORIZED, SUBSCRIPTION_INACTIVE, EVENT_EXPIRED and EVENT_TOO_LARGE. A skipped job may be terminal succeeded: inspect its code, not just the state.

 Network hardening and deployment

Default: public HTTPS only. All DNS A/AAAA answers must pass conservative IPv4/IPv6 special-range checks. The worker pins the validated IP while preserving the original Host/SNI and normal certificate verification, including on retries. Mapped IPv6, loopback, private/link-local, multicast, metadata and ambiguous IP forms do not bypass the default policy. No proxy or redirect path is used.

An operator may configure QB_WEBHOOK_INTERNAL_ALLOWLIST as a JSON map of exact HTTPS origins to fixed IPs, for example {"https://receiver.internal:8443":["10.20.0.5"]}. Set the same value on API and worker; Compose passes it to both. Entries are bounded to 20 origins/eight IPs each. There are no wildcards or insecure-TLS exceptions, and known metadata addresses remain prohibited. Treat allowlist changes as a reviewed trust-boundary change and enforce network-level egress rules too. Private certificates must be trusted through runtime/operator CA configuration, never a disabled verification flag.

The implementation follows [OWASP SSRF guidance](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Server_Side_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html) and uses the standard [HTTPS request API](https://nodejs.org/api/https.html). Local TLS/policy/replay tests do not certify every production receiver or network. Keep /internal/ inaccessible through the public gateway.

 Management contract

Prefix with /management/v1/projects/{projectId}/branches/{branchId}:

| Method | Route | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| GET | /webhooks | Metadata-only bounded subscription list. |
| POST | /webhooks | Create using name/url/serviceKeyId/table/events/columns; secret returned once. |
| POST | /webhooks/{id}/state | { state: "active" }, paused or revoked; revocation is permanent. |
| POST | /webhooks/{id}/rotate | Return one new signing secret with bounded overlap. |

The repository management client provides webhooks(projectId, branchId).list/create/state/rotate, retaining { data, meta }. Do not embed a management token or signing secret in browser application bundles. Creating a subscription is not an idempotent API: reconcile uncertain creation by listing its metadata before retrying.

 Passkeys and application MFA

 Availability and realm boundaries

Better Auth passkey and two-factor plugins are enabled for platform and application realms. Application credentials belong to an isolated branch; Studio credentials belong to the platform. They are not interchangeable. Browser SDK methods are available; native passkeys, Expo sessions and physical-device certification remain [expanded-release requirements](/docs/roadmap/native-push).

 Enroll and manage a browser passkey

Sign in first. Enrollment must run from a user action in a browser with WebAuthn support and an appropriate secure origin.


await client.auth.passkey.enroll({ name: 'Personal laptop' });
const passkeys = await client.auth.passkey.list();
console.log(passkeys);


The list currently returns unknown[]; validate entries before accessing their fields. client.auth.passkey.rename(id, name) updates a credential label and client.auth.passkey.remove(id) deletes that credential. Do not delete your last usable method without a recovery path.


await client.auth.passkey.signIn();
await client.auth.refreshSession();


Handle cancellation and PASSKEY_FAILED without repeatedly prompting the user. Passkey enrollment/sign-in is interactive, not a background task. Studio offers passkey management in account settings and Sign in with passkey on its sign-in screen.

 Origins and relying-party IDs

Platform WebAuthn uses the hostname of the first configured trusted origin (normally Studio) as its RP ID, falling back to the API URL; the configured platform origins are accepted assertion origins. Application WebAuthn uses its realm base URL's hostname and origin. A browser ceremony on an unrelated application domain is not automatically supported just because that domain is on the HTTP origin allowlist.

Keep deployment URLs stable and verify the intended browser ceremony before adoption. Changing the RP hostname does not migrate existing credentials. Native association documents and verified native assertion origins are not implemented yet.

 Enable application-user TOTP

An authenticated user supplies their current password. Present the enrollment URI securely and let them save the one-time backup codes outside the application database.


const enrollment = await client.auth.twoFactor.enable(password);
// Display enrollment.totpURI and backupCodes only to the enrolling user.
await client.auth.twoFactor.verify('123456'); // Replace with their current authenticator code.
await client.auth.refreshSession();


Do not log the enrollment URI, codes or passwords. verify completes enrollment or an outstanding login challenge; refresh the session afterward. disable(password) requires the current password.

 Complete a login challenge

Password sign-in throws QBError with code MFA_REQUIRED when a TOTP challenge is needed. This is not an authenticated session. Ask for the user's code, call auth.twoFactor.verify(code), then auth.refreshSession() before enabling protected queries. The challenge depends on the auth cookie flow, so cross-origin browser cookie restrictions still apply.

React applications use the same client methods; the provider/hook observes refreshed session state. There is no separate React-only passkey implementation. Keep [administrator MFA](/docs/authentication/administrator-mfa) enabled: adding a passkey does not disable QB_REQUIRE_ADMIN_MFA or satisfy a TOTP enrollment gate by itself.

 Upload and download files

 Configure any S3-compatible backend

QB Flex no longer bundles or starts MinIO. Provision RustFS or another S3-compatible provider separately, then configure the API with server-only operator credentials:


QB_S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9000
QB_S3_REGION=us-east-1
QB_S3_BUCKET=qb-flex
QB_S3_ACCESS_KEY=replace_with_provider_access_key
QB_S3_SECRET_KEY=replace_with_provider_secret_key


The endpoint above is for native development. Inside Compose, 127.0.0.1 points to that container, not the host; use a reachable service/remote endpoint or http://host.docker.internal:9000 with the host-gateway mapping. Use HTTPS for a remote production backend. Operator credentials never enter the browser SDK.

The storage-init service attempts physical-bucket provisioning. If your provider's credentials cannot create buckets, provision the physical bucket separately and grant the required object access. A logical bucket in Studio is not the physical backend bucket. Provider compatibility must be verified on your actual deployment.

Create logical buckets in Studio with name, visibility, size limit and optional MIME allowlist.

Studio refreshes file lists and related cached workspaces automatically after successful uploads/deletions. File-action menus remain non-modal; destructive confirmation dialogs still trap focus and support Escape. Cancellation restores focus to the opener; deleting its row moves focus to file search. Closing or submitting a confirmation must leave the rest of Studio interactive. See [submission refresh behavior](/docs/studio/workspacesautomatic-refresh-after-submissions).

 Upload lifecycle and streaming


const result = await qb.storage.upload(bucketId, 'notes/report.txt', file);
console.log(result.data.id);


The SDK creates an intent and uploads bytes through the authenticated gateway. Both management and application paths stream to private disk staging with backpressure and incremental native SHA-256, then stream validated bytes to S3. The object becomes ready only after backend persistence and expected-size verification. Failed/pending objects remain unavailable; retry/reconcile uncertain remote effects rather than assuming success.

Uploads are limited to 100 MiB, with at most four concurrent transfers and a 120-second deadline. Staging needs the expected size plus 64 MiB disk headroom. Files use mode 0600 in private directories (0700). Recognized abandoned staging is cleaned on startup only after exclusive volume ownership. Disk/WAL/backup capacity still needs operator monitoring. Resumable multipart uploads are not implemented.

 Optional expected checksum and cancellation

storage.upload accepts a lowercase 64-character SHA-256 digest. For a small browser file, this example uses Web Crypto; it buffers the file locally and is not a large-file streaming hash implementation.


const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', await file.arrayBuffer());
const checksum = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), byte = byte.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');
const upload = await client.storage.upload(bucketId, 'notes/report.txt', file, {
  checksum,
  signal: controller.signal,
});
console.log(upload.data.id);


Abort the controller to cancel. Size mismatch returns UPLOAD_MISMATCH; a digest mismatch returns CHECKSUM_MISMATCH before S3 PUT. Staging is removed on cancellation/failure. Existing clients without checksum metadata remain compatible. Browser hash-wasm workers and image optimization are [planned optional capabilities](/docs/roadmap/wasm), not requirements for uploading today.

 Ownership and downloads

Only authenticated application users can upload. Private objects are read/modified by their owners; public buckets allow reads of ready objects but retain owner-restricted writes. The current session and metadata owner check apply on every request. Studio uses separate audited organization capabilities, not application identity.


const blob = await qb.storage.download(objectId);
const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
// Use the URL in your UI, then release it:
URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
await qb.storage.remove(objectId);


Deletion marks metadata as deleting before removing backend bytes; it is finalized only after cleanup succeeds. Private responses use no-store. Keys are normalized and project/branch object prefixes are independent.

 Recovery boundaries

Database backups include object metadata, not bytes. Protect/version/back up your object provider separately. Restored metadata cannot assume the new branch prefix contains source bytes; perform explicit object recovery before expecting availability. See [backups](/docs/self-hosting/backups).

 React quickstart

 Before you begin

Create the task table, ownership policies and publishable key from [your first project](/docs/getting-started/first-project). Use React 19. The runnable example is in examples/react.


bun run --cwd examples/react dev


For an external application, run bun run sdk:pack in QB Flex, then install both artifacts and their React peers from your Vite React application:


npm install /path/to/QBFlex/dist/packages/qb-flex-client-0.1.0.tgz /path/to/QBFlex/dist/packages/qb-flex-react-0.1.0.tgz react@19.3.0 react-dom@19.3.0


Replace the paths with the actual absolute paths. npm installs the adapter's pinned TanStack Query dependency. Bun 1.4.2 attempts to resolve the unpublished client peer from the registry when installing these local tarballs, so use npm for this external artifact workflow. Configure the application's exact origin in Studio.

 Create the client once


import { createClient } from '@qb-flex/client';
import { QBProvider } from '@qb-flex/react';
import type { Database } from './qb-flex.types';

const client = createClientDatabase({
  url: 'http://localhost:7612',
  projectId: 'YOUR_PROJECT_ID',
  branch: 'primary',
  publishableKey: 'YOUR_PUBLISHABLE_KEY',
});

export function App() {
  return QBProvider client={client}Tasks //QBProvider;
}


The provider supplies a shared client and its own TanStack Query cache. You may pass an existing queryClient when integrating into an application that already uses Query.

 Read data with a custom hook


import { useQBQuery } from '@qb-flex/react';

function Tasks() {
  const query = useQBQuery({
    queryKey: ['tasks'],
    queryFn: (_, signal) = client.from('tasks')
      .select(['id', 'title'])
      .abortSignal(signal)
      .execute(),
  });
  if (query.isPending) return pLoading tasks…/p;
  if (query.error) return p role="alert"{query.error.message}/p;
  return ul{query.data.data.map(task =
    li key={task.id}{task.title}/li
  )}/ul;
}


Use generated database declarations to retain column and row inference. Include filters and sort choices in queryKey whenever they affect the result.

 Next steps

Add [authentication](/docs/sdk/react/authentication), [mutations and realtime](/docs/sdk/react/data), then [file handling](/docs/sdk/react/storage). Every hook requires the provider; a missing provider produces a clear configuration error.

 React authentication

 Read the shared session


import { useQBAuth } from '@qb-flex/react';

function Account() {
  const auth = useQBAuth();
  if (auth.isLoading) return pChecking your session…/p;
  if (!auth.user) return SignIn /;
  return section
    pSigned in as {auth.user.email}/p
    button onClick={() = auth.signOut().catch(console.error)}Sign out/button
  /section;
}


All components under the provider observe the same session. The hook exposes session, user, loading/error state and signIn/signUp/signOut/refresh actions.

 Sign in or register


await auth.signIn({ email, password });
await auth.signUp({ email, password, name });


Catch failures and present auth.error.message in the form. Registration may require verification before a session is available. Never assume signup always authenticates the user.

 Enable protected queries


const auth = useQBAuth();
const tasks = useQBQuery({
  queryKey: ['tasks'],
  enabled: Boolean(auth.user),
  queryFn: (_, signal) = client.from('tasks').abortSignal(signal).execute(),
});


Conditional rendering improves the interface; server policies are still the authorization boundary.

 Identity changes

Query keys include the project, branch and identity generation. The provider cancels in-flight queries and removes affected cached results when the user changes. Realtime subscriptions reauthorize under the new session.

Do not keep copies of protected query data in unrelated global state. Clear application-specific derived state on signout as well.

 Server rendering

Create clients and QueryClient instances per request. Do not reuse a module-global authenticated client on a server. Hooks do not read browser APIs during server rendering; session loading occurs after initialization. Advanced request-session hydration requires an explicit integration rather than assuming browser cookies exist on the server.

 Passkeys and TOTP

Use the provider's shared client for auth.passkey and auth.twoFactor, then refresh its session to update the hook. Do not invent passkey methods on useQBAuth; its actions retain their existing signatures. Catch MFA_REQUIRED from password sign-in, collect a TOTP code, verify it through the client and refresh before enabling protected queries. See [browser passkeys and MFA](/docs/authentication/passkeys-mfa).

 React queries, mutations and realtime

 Query ownership

The query function receives the configured client and a cancellation signal. A query key must include every input that changes the data. The provider prefixes it with project, branch and identity scope.

 Insert a task


const auth = useQBAuth();
const addTask = useQBMutation({
  mutationFn: (_, title: string) = client.from('tasks').insert({
    title,
    owner_id: auth.user!.id,
  }, { idempotencyKey: crypto.randomUUID() }),
  invalidate: [['tasks']],
});

await addTask.mutateAsync('Write the first integration');


The hook exposes TanStack Query mutation state, including pending/error state. Generate the idempotency key once for each logical action; keep it when retrying that action manually.

 Update or delete


await client.from('tasks').update(task.id, { completed: 1 }, {
  revision: Number(task.revision),
});
await client.from('tasks').delete(task.id);


Wrap these operations in useQBMutation and invalidate the task query after success. A stale revision returns a conflict; refetch and let the user reconcile rather than overwriting blindly.

 Subscribe through a hook


const realtime = useQBRealtime({
  table: 'tasks',
  enabled: Boolean(auth.user),
  cursor: tasks.data?.meta.consistencyToken,
  onEvent: () = {},
  invalidate: [['tasks']],
});


The hook creates and disposes the subscription with the component lifecycle. It invalidates the selected queries when events arrive and exposes connection status. React Strict Mode cleanup must not leave a duplicate connection.

 Loading and failure states

Show empty lists distinctly from policy denial or network errors. Disable duplicate mutation submissions while pending. Keep user drafts when a write fails. Use query refetch for recoverable failures; denied writes need a changed identity, policy or input.

 React file handling

 Use the storage hook


import { useQBStorage } from '@qb-flex/react';

function Upload({ bucketId }: { bucketId: string }) {
  const storage = useQBStorage();
  return labelAttach a file
    input type="file" disabled={storage.isPending} onChange={async event = {
      const file = event.target.files?.[0];
      if (!file) return;
      try { await storage.upload(bucketId, file.name, file); }
      catch { / Render storage.error in the form. / }
    }} /
    {storage.error && span role="alert"{storage.error.message}/span}
  /label;
}


isPending describes transfer activity, not byte progress. Initial uploads are bounded, non-resumable transfers. Keep filename and size visible while the transfer runs.

 Download safely

Call storage.download(objectId) to receive a Blob. Create an object URL for the browser and revoke it after use. Do not expose an S3 backend URL or credentials.

 Delete and refresh

Call storage.remove(objectId) and invalidate the file-list query after success. Listing uses the same client identity. An object from another private owner is unavailable.

 Cancellation

Upload/download methods accept a request option containing an AbortSignal. Cancel operations when the user deliberately leaves the transfer flow. Cancellation does not guarantee that remote work did not commit; refresh object state before retrying.

 Upload integrity and limits

The shared upload method accepts optional SHA-256 checksum and AbortSignal options. Server uploads stream through private disk staging before S3 persistence, with size/checksum verification, four-transfer concurrency and a 100 MiB maximum. This does not add resumable or native background uploads. See [storage configuration and integrity examples](/docs/storage/files).

 JavaScript authentication and CRUD

 Sign in and observe session state


await qb.auth.signUp({ email, password, name });
await qb.auth.signIn({ email, password });
const stopAuth = qb.onAuthStateChange(session = {
  accountLabel.textContent = session?.user.email ?? 'Signed out';
});
await qb.auth.refreshSession();


Always handle rejected promises and verification requirements. Sessions are shared by calls through the same client instance. SDK tokens are not automatically written to localStorage.

 Load tasks


const result = await qb.from('tasks')
  .select(['id', 'title', 'completed'])
  .eq('completed', 0)
  .limit(50);

list.replaceChildren(...result.data.map(task = {
  const item = document.createElement('li');
  item.textContent = task.title;
  return item;
}));


Use textContent for application values instead of inserting user-provided HTML.

 Insert, update and delete


const session = qb.getSession();
if (!session) throw new Error('Sign in first');
await qb.from('tasks').insert({
  owner_id: session.user.id,
  title: 'Ship the first application',
}, { idempotencyKey: crypto.randomUUID() });
await qb.from('tasks').update(taskId, { completed: 1 });
await qb.from('tasks').delete(taskId);


Ownership is validated by the server. Refetch the list after mutations so sorting, filters and counts remain consistent.

 Pagination and cancellation

Follow result.meta.cursor with the exact same query configuration. Use abortSignal(controller.signal) for a pending read and call controller.abort() when it is no longer needed.

 Error handling


try { await qb.from('tasks').insert(row); }
catch (error) { errorMessage.textContent = error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Request failed'; }


Keep form input on failure. Resolve validation and policy errors before retrying. For a potentially committed mutation, reuse the original idempotency key rather than creating another action.

 JavaScript realtime and files

 Subscribe after an authorized snapshot


const snapshot = await qb.from('tasks');
const channel = qb.channel({
  table: 'tasks',
  cursor: snapshot.meta.consistencyToken,
  onEvent: () = refreshTasks(),
  onStatus: status = { connectionLabel.textContent = status; },
  onResync: () = refreshTasks(),
}).subscribe();


A subscription is an explicit resource. Dispose it when replacing the screen or leaving the application. Realtime delivery uses the current session and exposed-table policies.

 Upload a file


const file = fileInput.files?.[0];
if (file) {
  const uploaded = await qb.storage.upload(bucketId, file.name, file);
  const blob = await qb.storage.download(uploaded.data.id);
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  downloadLink.href = url;
  downloadLink.download = file.name;
  // Release after the UI no longer uses the URL:
  URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
}


Use an authenticated application user and a configured S3 backend. Private files are restricted to their owner.

 Lifecycle cleanup


channel.unsubscribe();
stopAuth();
qb.dispose();


Call cleanup only when the instance is no longer used. dispose closes channels and removes session listeners. A disposed client rejects later requests.

 Identity changes

Refetch and clear protected DOM state after signout. Keep every application's state scoped to its client and user; do not mix results from another project. The SDK reauthorizes its channels after identity changes, while your application remains responsible for clearing previously rendered private content.

 Upload integrity and limits

The shared upload method accepts optional SHA-256 checksum and AbortSignal options. Server uploads stream through private disk staging before S3 persistence, with size/checksum verification, four-transfer concurrency and a 100 MiB maximum. This does not add resumable or native background uploads. See [storage configuration and integrity examples](/docs/storage/files).

